is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage. Both “floor” VLANs (10.5.0.x = VLAN 5 and 10.6.0.x = VLAN 6) have working default gateways and upstream routing right now.

Evidence:
- From a 10.5 host (pc1), the default gateway 10.5.0.254 replies (3/3 OK), and upstream 55.5.1.1 is reachable (2/2 OK). From a 10.6 host (pc3), the default gateway 10.6.0.254 replies (3/3 OK) and 55.5.1.1 is reachable (2/2 OK).
- On the Campus 2 access switch (switch4), the trunk to the distribution (eth0) carries dot1q VLAN subinterfaces eth0.5 and eth0.6 which are up and forwarding and bridged to br_vlan5 and br_vlan6 alongside the correct access ports (brctl/bridge/ip -d link evidence).
- On the distribution switch (sshswitch), matching subinterfaces eth0.5 and eth0.6 are up and in br_v5/br_v6, which host the SVI gateways 10.5.0.254 and 10.6.0.254 (ip addr/brctl evidence).
- Upstream routing from the distribution is present via OSPF (routes via 55.5.1.1 visible in ip route), and cross-VLAN/remote reachability tests succeed topology-wide.

Mechanism if the reported symptom had persisted would have been a missing VLAN on the trunk (eth0↔eth0), but both VLANs 5 and 6 are currently present end-to-end. Localization: switch4.eth0 trunk and sshswitch.eth0 trunk subinterfaces are correct and operational. Root cause: no fault found at this time; likely a transient interruption during maintenance that has been restored. Corrective fix: none required now; keep VLANs 5 and 6 permitted on the trunk, and implement monitoring/change validation to catch any future trunk pruning immediately. 
=== END ===